single segment. A genotype with lifespan fraction l_g keeps the same
thresholds in degree-days; only the segment in which its lifespan l_g \code{adult_lifespan_dd}
ends changes length. A genotype whose lifespan ends before a threshold
never reaches it (reported by \code{print()}).

\strong{State layout.} All states are counts per node, stored in one vector of
blocks E, L, P, M, U, F (in this order). Within each block the index order
from slowest to fastest is node, host class, genotype, mate genotype
(F only), sub-stage. Use \code{\link[=sg_state_index]{sg_state_index()}} and \code{\link[=sg_state_names]{sg_state_names()}} rather
than computing positions by hand.

\strong{Several nodes} (D-032). With a landscape of N nodes the state vector is
the concatenation of N per-node vectors, each with the layout above (node
slowest), and state names end in \code{"@"} and the node id, e.g.
\code{"M[AA,1]@north"}. Each node's wound count (or host count), mate-finding
half-saturation and per-km^2 releases use that node's area and densities.
Releases need a \code{node} (\code{\link[=sg_releases]{sg_releases()}}).

\strong{Releases} enter the first sub-stage of their stage: eggs E, pupae P,
adult males M and adult females U (unmated).

\strong{Population size from \code{adult_females}} (D-028). The parameters set the
population density (per km^2); the node area sets how many individuals
that is. With \code{adult_females}, the area is the target divided by the
equilibrium density of adult females at the forcing's temperature (from
\code{\link[=sg_init]{sg_init()}} with \code{method = "equilibrium"}). Densities in \code{params}
(\code{wound_density}, \code{male_density_half_mating}) stay as given, so the
number of wounds in the node (\code{wound_density} times the area, or with the
host-wound model the number of hosts, \code{host_density} times the area)
scales with the region. Every rate of the model depends on counts only
through densities, so the equilibrium is exactly proportional to the
area, also with larval crowding or an Allee effect. \code{print()} shows the
area chosen.

\strong{Fingerprint.} \code{model$meta$hash} is an MD5 hash of all model inputs
(parameters, cube, forcing, releases, area and adult chains), so two models
with the same hash give the same simulation.
